/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Internal energy U is the energy stored inside the boundary: the kinetic and potential energy of the molecules themselves, with the system at rest as a whole.
   - Enthalpy is defined as H = U + PV, where P is pressure in Pa and V is volume in m³.
   - Why add PV?
   - Heat capacity is the heat needed to raise a unit amount by one kelvin.
   - Two slopes exist because the paths differ.
   - Qp is the heat added at constant pressure, in J; ΔU is the internal energy change, in J; P is the constant pressure, in Pa; ΔV is the volume change, in m³; ΔH is the enthalpy change, in J.
   - So for the same kind of process, Qp = ΔH.
   - Joule's experiment (1.5) found no measurable temperature change when air at low pressure expanded into vacuum with no work and no heat.
   - Differentiate h = u + RT with respect to T at constant pressure: the R term contributes R, so Cp = Cv + R.
   - Cp is the molar heat capacity at constant pressure, in J/(mol·K); Cv is the molar heat capacity at constant volume, in J/(mol·K); R = 8.314 J/(mol·K); γ (gamma) is the heat capacity ratio, dimensionless.
*/
