/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Per kilogram passing a steady device, with c the speed, q the heat added and ws the shaft work done by the fluid:
   - h in kJ/kg; c in m/s; z in m; g = 9.81 m/s². c²/2 is in J/kg: divide it by 1000 before adding it to h in kJ/kg.
   - A throttling valve drops the pressure with no work and no heat, so h₁ = h₂.
   - A nozzle speeds the fluid up; a diffuser slows it down.
   - For a reversible steady-flow process with kinetic and potential terms neglected, the shaft work put into the fluid is win = ∫v dP from P₁ to P₂: the area to the left of the path on a P–v plot, not the area under it, which is the closed-syst
   - win is work into the gas per kg; R = 8.314/M kJ/(kg·K), 0.287 for air; T₂s is the isentropic exit temperature.
   - Isothermal compression needs the least work and isentropic the most of the reversible paths with 1 < n < γ.
   - Water keeps its density in a pipe; a gas does not, because its pressure and density fall as it speeds up.
   - dA and dc are small changes of area and speed along the duct; Ma is the local Mach number.
   - Below Ma = 1 the flow speeds up only where the duct narrows; above Ma = 1 it speeds up only where the duct widens; Ma = 1 can occur only at the narrowest section, the throat.
*/
