/* book Worked example 2: Q: An uncooled compressor takes 0.50 kg/s of air at 1.00 bar and 300 K and delivers it at 5.00 bar. Its isentropic efficiency is 0.80. Take cp = 1.005 kJ/(kg·K), γ = 1.4 and R = 0.287 kJ/(kg·K), and neglect kinetic terms. Find the isentropic exit temperature, the actual work and exit temperature, and the power; compare with reversible isothermal compression.
   1. Pressure ratio 5.00; exponent (γ − 1)/γ = 0.4/1.4 = 0.2857; 5.000.2857 = 1.5838.
   2. Isentropic exit: T₂s = 300 × 1.5838 = 475.1 K, and wisentropic = cp(T₂s − T₁) = 1.005 × 175.1 = 176.0 kJ/kg.
   3. Actual work: wactual = wisentropic/ηc = 176.0/0.80 = 220.0 kJ/kg, so T₂ = T₁ + wactual/cp = 300 + 220.0/1.005 = 518.9 K.
   4. Power: ṁ wactual = 0.50 × 220.0 = 110.0 kW (into the air).
   5. Reversible isothermal: win = RT₁ ln 5 = 0.287 × 300 × 1.6094 = 138.6 kJ/kg, power 0.50 × 138.6 = 69.3 kW.
   Answer:  */
