/* book exam traps: Adding c²/2 in m²/s² straight to h in kJ/kg. Divide the kinetic term by 1000 first: 1 kJ/kg = 1000 m²/s². | Using the closed-system work ∫P dv for a flow compressor. The steady-flow work is ∫v dP; for a polytropic path the two differ by the factor n. | Reversing the compressor sign. In the equation ws is work done by the fluid, so for a compressor ws = h₁ − h₂ is negative; the work input is h₂ − h₁. | Expecting a converging nozzle to give a supersonic jet. It reaches at most Ma = 1 at its exit; supersonic flow needs a diverging part after the throat. | Using T in °C, or R = 8.314 J/(mol·K), in a = √(γRT); use K and 287 J/(kg·K) for air. */
